<edge source="strait_hormuz_standoff::incident_rate" target="currency_market_shock::market_liquidity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A spike in hostile maritime incidents increases counterparty and operational risk for traders and market-makers, prompting wider spreads and reduced willingness to warehouse positions which reduces market liquidity.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
  <data key="d39">currency_market_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="strait_hormuz_standoff::incident_rate" target="global_ai_competition::financial_liquidity_ai" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A spike in maritime incidents raises insurance premia and market volatility which prompts institutional and venture investors to pull back or delay funding rounds for higher‑burn AI firms, reducing available liquidity for AI organizations.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
  <data key="d39">global_ai_competition</data>
</edge>
<edge source="strait_hormuz_standoff::incident_rate" target="russia_ukraine_war::interceptor_stock_levels" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A sustained rise in hostile incidents against commercial and naval vessels compels NATO/partner forces to redeploy and actively employ expendable interceptors to defend ships and forward bases in the Gulf, depleting inventories available for resupply to the Euro‑Atlantic air‑defense system.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">strait_hormuz_standoff</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
